The first unified development controller executes actual context, semantic,
symbolic, scorer and world-memory operations through explicitly separate source
profiles. Its frozen `controller_qualification/development-v2` run repaired **two
of four initial constructed tasks**. The other two remain `residual_pending`;
the provider was disabled and no model response or successful repair was invented.
This is partial integration qualification, not completed real-repository E06.

Across thirteen prescribed events, nine became locally accepted state, two
remained pending residuals, one lost the local ledger's parent CAS, and one
retained an unsettled local execution. Sixteen actual controller processes
included three abrupt interruptions and three fresh restarts. The immutable
controller snapshot, full profile, subprocess/effect receipts, SQLite ledger,
accepted source files and audit are in `controller_qualification/development-v2`.
The profile freezes this controller's bytes, so future changes require a new
run/profile rather than silently extending these results.

| Actually executed operation | Count |
| --- | ---: |
| Native context reads | 8 |
| Underlying verified native source expansions | 16 |
| Native source snapshots and verified semantic preparations | 13 |
| Native IPA/CEGIS attempts | 8 |
| Actual native candidate sources produced | 6 |
| Native routing decisions | 13 |
| Native world-memory service operations | 19 (11 propose, 8 record) |
| Compiler-selected actions dispatched and checked | 3 |
| Isolated scorer attempts | 30 |
| Durably retained scorer terminal receipts | 29 |
| Provider dispatches / provider tokens | 0 / 0 |
| Native sealing/publication operations | 0 |

The successful scorer receipts report 154 observed function calls; failed
checks and the deliberately unsettled scorer attempt do not have complete
function-call totals. Thus 154 is a recorded subset, not an estimate of all
execution work. The thirteen events used 91 durable effect identities and
63.934931 seconds across controller invocations. That wall time includes the
service subprocesses and restart invocations; it excludes earlier context-pack
priming, runtime-source inventory preparation, implementation and audit. No
full-workflow speedup or token-savings estimate follows from these measurements.

Each initial fixture comes from the pre-existing `routing_v2/cases.json` order:
`unobserved_status`, `swallowed_backend_error`, `clamp_interval`, and
`stable_deduplication`. A separately enrolled context service reads the first
three public fields—source, objective and visible validation—from native
Datasets/Kit packs. It expands actual stored raw source bytes, checks current
source and objective identity, and returns JSON. The controller then supplies
those returned bytes to the semantic/symbolic worker. The held-back constructed
audit validation is not projected into the context producer or symbolic request.
These remain known constructed tasks, not hidden historical repairs or fresh
holdouts.

The semantic worker uses normal current-external package imports to acquire the
complete declared single-file implementation snapshot, scan native symbols and
dependencies, construct and verify a native semantic-state bundle, retain raw
source and unknown-frontier records, and bind an explicit behavioral obligation.
The objective and behavioral obligation were authored for the evaluation; this
does not qualify autonomous specification inference or complete plan coverage.
Native CEGIS proposes actual changed source only for its two applicable defect
grammars. Its internal marker gates are never counted as theorem proofs or
independent test execution. A real isolated scorer checks visible behavior and
then a separately supplied audit. Candidate code receives typed call inputs in
a worker process and never receives oracle source.

Native `route_model` consumes the actual closure status with zero available
proofs. Its context-size input is a labelled `ceil(raw UTF-8 bytes / 4)` heuristic,
not provider token telemetry. The two unsupported tasks reach the real common
residual interface and persist pending request identities. Cheap abstention is
not counted as successful reasoning or an efficiency improvement.

After successful validation, the controller records a native world-memory
observation for the checked current candidate bytes. On a subsequent event it
loads the source from the locally accepted parent's immutable file, obtains a
native compiler proposal, resolves candidate and observation by the compiler's
actual returned CIDs, and dispatches the selected current-validation operation.
It still executes both checks. An ordinary control also gets the same cheap
current-source no-op check. This single-action continuation demonstrates actual
service consumption and equal baseline opportunity, not learned world-model
advantage, semantic generalization, execution-proof reuse or cached-output
acceptance. A real comment-only source edit rejects the old world candidate as
stale and triggers current checking. Parent-source continuations are preserved
raw and rescanned; their changed-source context-pack rebinding is not enrolled.

The effect ledger uses SQLite WAL with `synchronous=FULL`, immutable request and
completion files, and a hash-linked append-only event journal. An effect identity
binds event, stage and exact request before dispatch. A repeated event/stage
cannot silently change its request. Terminal effects return their hash-verified
stored receipts; an interrupted attempt with no durable receipt remains unknown
and is not dispatched again.

| Actual interruption/control | Measured continuation |
| --- | --- |
| Exit 91 after candidate validation, before memory record/local acceptance | Fresh process reused completed effect receipts and finished the event without rerunning validation |
| Exit 93 after durable audit completion, before effect-ledger terminal update | Fresh process reconciled the stored completion and finished the event |
| Exit 92 after the actual audit subprocess returned, before any durable process/completion receipt | Fresh process retained `effect_unknown`; the audit effect had one dispatch intent and was not repeated |
| Valid stale-parent branch | Actual local SQLite CAS rejected it; the current `status-comment` root at generation 2 remained unchanged |

These are deliberate process exits at named boundaries, not filesystem-fault,
power-loss or concurrent stress tests. The accepted pointer is an evaluation
ledger record. **It is not native Kit root publication or E08 sealing.** World
records are unsigned declarations of checked execution by this local controller;
they do not confer production promotion authority. A stale branch may retain
valid candidate observations while failing the independent accepted-state CAS.

`MeteredResidual.request` is the only neural interface and delegates to the
existing `evals/metered_provider.py` transport. It registers purpose-specific
identities for planning, formalization, source expansion, proposal, review,
recovery and retry; every enabled call must be present in a frozen provider
profile. The ledger persists its intent before that transport can dispatch,
retains authoritative usage/charge fields, and can reconcile the transport's
own terminal receipt after interruption. A truly unknown provider effect would
retain unknown usage rather than zero. Only **proposal-needed, provider-disabled
requests** were exercised here. The other purposes and actual provider dispatch
are interface capabilities, not counted integrated observations. Enabling them
requires a new unified source/case/provider freeze.

Three namespace profiles remain separate subprocesses:

| Service | Bound implementation |
| --- | --- |
| Context materialization | `context_qualification/context_read_v1.py` and `routing_enrollment_v1/freeze.json`; its native expansions use the independently qualified context profile |
| Semantic analysis, CEGIS and route | Current external accelerate `5d79bbf8bb61e027fbda76920580cb9d4fd919c1`, datasets `ac82107e246b30e35a2bbdcf75e01370d22350c6`, kit `6196017ca3df016c7159dce43af60f2a0d96a9ae` |
| World-memory compilation and storage | `world_memory_development/service.py` with the explicitly patched `compiler_identity_v1` profile, not a mixed import namespace |

The profile contains 6,815 current-external Python file hashes and separate
world-service import hashes. The audit verified 309 actually loaded semantic
and world-module files against those frozen inventories. Context runtime bytes
are independently pinned and checked by its own enrolled expansion service.
`controller_qualification/development-v2/audit.json` passed **9,188** checks of
actual subprocess results, context/source bindings, candidate bytes, current
execution, accepted parent consumption, journal/effect hashes and recovery.

Failures and iterations are preserved. `preflight-v1` hit an enclosing Git-tree
scan instead of the declared single-file scope; setting Git's ceiling to the
source directory's parent fixed it in `preflight-v2`. The first qualification
inventory found a directory ending in `.py`; `development-v1` stopped before
freezing or dispatching and retains its runner/controller snapshots. The final
runner inventories regular files. The first read-only audit assumed full
candidate source in the CEGIS serialization; its failed source/receipt remain
as `audit-attempt-v1.*`. The corrected audit checks the native source hash and
the IPA receipt's actual source bytes. It did not modify or rerun controller
outcomes.

The executable entry point is:

```sh
.venv/bin/python papers/revisions/neurosymbolic_system_scope_20260914/evals/integrated_v1/controller.py run --profile ABS_PROFILE_JSON --event FROZEN_EVENT_ID
```

It returns one JSON event result. Its internal fresh-process service protocol is
`controller.py worker`, with a JSON request on stdin whose role is `prepare`,
`symbolic`, `route`, or `score`. Context's repeatable CLI returns a verified JSON
source/objective projection. World's stdin JSON API supports `record` and
`propose`. All service entrypoints, exact requests, outputs and source digests
are retained under the event's effect identity.

Native sealing and real-repository source/objective execution are the next
integration steps. A sealing adapter must receive the complete event/receipt
inventory, actual checked source/obligation identities and independently read
expected native parent, then return actual native seal/store/CAS/recovery
receipts. No sealing stub or enum branch exists in this controller and none is
counted above. The current scorer worker only supports the four constructed
routing tasks; a public repository objective and a separately qualified real
candidate scorer need a new task adapter and freeze. Full real-repository E06,
integrated native publication, learned-memory/procedure effects, the metered
provider trajectory and confirmatory token savings all remain open.
